.\" groff man page for the retiring bn compatibility dispatcher (0.6 line).
.TH BN 1 "September 22, 2026" "bn 0.6" "Basic Next"
.SH NAME
bn \- compatibility dispatcher for bni and bnc (retires in 0.7)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bn
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.I source-or-file
.RI [ "-- program-args" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Basic Next 0.6 ships two executables:
.BR bni (1),
the interpreter (with
.BR check ,
.BR lex ,
.BR eval ,
.B lsp
and
.BR dap ),
and
.BR bnc (1),
the compiler.
.B bn
keeps the 0.5 command line working for one minor release by forwarding to
the executable installed next to it:
.TP
.BI "bn " run|eval|check|lex|lsp|dap " ..."
runs
.BI "bni " command " ..."
unchanged, except that the 0.5 compiler flags
.B --target
and
.BR --opt ,
which
.B bn
accepted and ignored on these commands, are dropped.
.TP
.BI "bn build " "[options] file"
runs
.BI "bnc " "[options] file"
unchanged.
.PP
Standard input, output and error are passed through, and the exit status is
the forwarded program's. When standard error is a terminal,
.B bn
prints one deprecation line there first; on a pipe it prints nothing, so
.B "bn eval --format json"
still owns both channels.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B BN_DISPATCH
(exit 2): the sibling executable could not be started. Install
.B bni
and
.B bnc
next to
.BR bn ,
or on
.BR PATH .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bni (1),
.BR bnc (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Carlos Quintella, Basic Next BDFL.
